= Schisandra

_Scope: This entry treats_ schisandra _—_ Schisandra chinensis _(Turcz.) Baill. (Schisandraceae), the canonical Chinese 五味子 (wǔwèizǐ, "five-flavor seed") of the_ Shennong Bencao Jing _top grade. The closely-related southern schisandra_ Schisandra sphenanthera _Rehder & E. H. Wilson (南五味子 nán wǔwèizǐ) is a recognized but distinct drug — the modern Chinese Pharmacopoeia treats them as_ separate drugs _and they are not interchangeable; this entry treats the northern_ S. chinensis _as the principal subject, with explicit notes on_ S. sphenanthera _and the closely-related Pacific_ S. glabra _(rarely used). Status: classical in Chinese medicine (top grade in_ Shennong Bencao Jing_); not classical in Ayurveda, Tibb, or Greco-Roman traditions (an East Asian / Northeast Asian temperate herb); modern Korean and Russian / Soviet adaptogen register; well-documented modern phytopharmacology around the schisandrin family of lignans (hepatoprotective, CNS-modulating)._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Schisandra chinensis_ (Turcz.) Baill. (sometimes spelled _Schizandra_, the older form), family Schisandraceae. The drug is the *dried mature fruit* (_Schisandrae fructus_) — a small red berry on a climbing woody vine. _Schisandra sphenanthera_ Rehder & E. H. Wilson (southern schisandra, 南五味子) is a related Chinese species, distinguished in the modern Chinese Pharmacopoeia as a separate drug. _Schisandra glabra_ (a far-eastern North American species) is recognized but rarely used medicinally. The genus name *Schisandra* from Greek σχιστός (_schistós_, "split, divided") + ἀνήρ (_anḗr_, gen. ἀνδρός _andrós_, "man"), referring to the *split stamens* of the male flower — a botanical-morphological coinage by Michaux. The misspelling _Schizandra_ — common in older Western herbal literature — is a Latinization through German.#footnote[Etymology: standard botanical sources; the split-stamen morphology distinguishes the genus. The Michaux coinage is from the early 19th c. American botanical observation of the related _S. glabra_.]

*English:* _Schisandra_ — the standard botanical name; *Chinese magnolia vine*, *five-flavor berry*, *Northern five-flavor berry*, *magnolia berry*. The "magnolia" association is botanical: Schisandraceae was historically classified close to Magnoliaceae (both in basal angiosperm lineages), though modern phylogenetics (APG) places Schisandraceae in its own order (Austrobaileyales).#footnote[_OED_ (s.v. "schisandra"). The "five-flavor" descriptive name is a direct translation of the Chinese 五味子.]

*Chinese:* *五味子 (wǔwèizǐ)* — "five-flavor seed" — the canonical Chinese drug name, recorded in the *Shennong Bencao Jing* (神農本草經, Han dynasty) as a *top-grade drug (上品)*. The name describes the *unique sensory experience* of chewing the dried berry: the *five classical tastes* are all detectable — *sour* (predominant, 酸 suān), *bitter* (苦 kǔ), *sweet* (甘 gān), *pungent* (辛 xīn), and *salty* (鹹 xián). The five-flavor signature is a *pharmacological signature* in classical Chinese thought: a drug containing all five flavors is treated as having unusually broad and balanced action across multiple organ systems (the five-phase / 五行 wǔxíng correspondences). The northern (_S. chinensis_) and southern (_S. sphenanthera_) drugs are distinguished in modern pharmacopoeia: *北五味子 (běi wǔwèizǐ)* — northern, _S. chinensis_, the principal medicinal drug, darker and more sour; *南五味子 (nán wǔwèizǐ)* — southern, _S. sphenanthera_, somewhat lighter and used as a recognized but distinct drug.#footnote[五味子 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (top grade); systematized in _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The 北 / 南 wǔwèizǐ distinction is preserved in the modern _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ — the two are separate drugs and substitution is not routine.]

*Japanese:* *五味子 (gomishi)* — Japanese reading of the Chinese characters; the Japanese register uses 五味子 in the kampō tradition (Japanese-Chinese medicine) preserving the Chinese clinical use. Some Japanese folk-medicinal use of native Japanese magnolia-vine relatives exists but the canonical drug is the Chinese-imported one.

*Korean:* *오미자 (omija)* — Korean reading of the same characters; *omija-cha* (오미자차) — five-flavor berry tea, a traditional Korean drink, especially in Jeolla and Gangwon provinces; the herb is widely consumed in Korea as a folk-and-traditional-medicine beverage, distinctly more dietary in the Korean tradition than the Chinese (where it remains primarily a pharmaceutical drug).

*Russian / Soviet (the adaptogen register):* *Лимонник* (_Limonnik_, "lemon-plant") — for the Far-Eastern Russian Primorye populations of _S. chinensis_; the herb was studied extensively in 1950s–1960s Soviet pharmacology under Nikolai Lazarev and Israil Brekhman as part of the *adaptogen* research program (alongside ginseng, eleuthero, rhodiola, aralia), with documented effects on stamina, fatigue resistance, and stress-tolerance — providing some of the foundational Western-scientific data on the herb.#footnote[Soviet adaptogen literature: Lazarev's 1947 "adaptogen" concept, elaborated by Brekhman in the 1950s–1960s; studies on Soviet fighter-pilot, factory-worker, and athlete populations using schisandra and other adaptogens. The Russian-language literature is substantial; English-language summaries in modern adaptogen research (Panossian, Wikman, et al.).]

*Sanskrit / Arabic / Latin classical:* *Not classical in Ayurveda, Tibb, or Greco-Roman traditions.* Schisandra is a *Northeast Asian temperate vine* — outside the geographical range of the Indian, Arabic-Islamic, and Mediterranean classical pharmacopoeias. Modern Western and Indian use is *post-classical extension*.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

*五味子 is one of the most canonical and most clinically central Chinese drugs* — top-grade in the *Shennong Bencao Jing*, with continuous treatment through the entire Chinese medical tradition. The clinical register is *astringing, qi-and-yin-preserving, spirit-calming, fluid-generating* — fitting the *five-flavor signature* with its broad multi-organ action.#footnote[五味子 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_; elaborated in _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Warm (溫).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* *Sour (酸) primarily*, with the other four flavors (sweet 甘, bitter 苦, pungent 辛, salty 鹹) all present — *the five-flavor signature* from which the name derives.

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Lung (肺), Heart (心), Kidney (腎). The triple Lung-Heart-Kidney placement is the canonical reading, reflecting the multi-system action.

*Actions & indications:*
- *斂肺止咳 (liǎn fèi zhǐ ké)* — *astringes Lung qi, stops cough* — for chronic cough, asthma, and wheezing from Lung-qi-or-Lung-yin vacuity; one of the cardinal classical uses. The *cough-stopping* action is unusual for an *astringent* drug, since most cough-treatments rely on *moving qi* or *transforming phlegm* — schisandra is the classical *astringing* cough-treatment.
- *滋腎 (zī shèn)* and *固精止瀉 (gù jīng zhǐ xiè)* — *nourishes Kidney, stabilizes essence, stops diarrhea* — for seminal emission, spermatorrhea, chronic diarrhea (especially the canonical *cock-crow diarrhea*, 五更瀉 wǔ gēng xiè, of Kidney-yang vacuity), urinary frequency, leukorrhea.
- *斂汗 (liǎn hàn)* — *stops sweating* — for spontaneous sweating (qi vacuity) and night sweats (yin vacuity); classically used with 浮小麥 (floating wheat) for vacuity sweating.
- *生津 (shēng jīn)* — *generates fluids* — for thirst, fluid-depletion, *xiāokě* (consumptive thirst, the classical category that includes diabetes mellitus and other fluid-depleting conditions); used in the classical 生脈散 (Shēng Mài Sǎn, "Generate-Pulse Powder") with 人參 ginseng and 麥門冬 ophiopogon — for qi-and-fluid depletion from heat injury or chronic exhaustion.
- *寧心安神 (níng xīn ān shén)* — *quiets the Heart, calms the spirit* — for palpitations, insomnia, anxiety, especially with concurrent Heart-yin or Heart-qi vacuity.
- *Modern hepatoprotective register:* 五味子 has been extensively used in modern Chinese hepatology for *chronic hepatitis*, especially with elevated ALT/AST — the schisandrin family of lignans (see Modern Pharmacology) is responsible. Classical use for *Liver qi disorders* extends naturally into modern hepatoprotective applications.

*Cautions:* Externally-contracted febrile disease (the astringent action would lock in the pathogen); cough or asthma with excessive phlegm or external invasion (astringing inappropriate); inflammatory skin conditions; pregnancy at high therapeutic doses (caution for strong-acting herbs). *Initial-stage acute illness — the astringing action is contraindicated*.#footnote[Actions and cautions: 五味子 in _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; standard modern Chinese pharmacology texts. The astringing-on-cough contraindication in acute external-contraction conditions is a classical clinical commonplace.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Schisandra is not classical in Ayurveda._ The reading would be *reasoned extension* — the unusual *five-flavor* signature would place it conceptually with *tridoṣa-pacifying* herbs (those that pacify all three doṣas, sharing rasa-coverage across the five flavors); the sour-warming profile would suggest *Vāta-Kapha-pacifying*; the chronic adaptogenic / rasāyana register is plausible by extension. Modern post-classical Ayurvedic literature has integrated schisandra as a *rasāyana-class adaptogen* alongside other modern integrations (e.g. rhodiola, eleuthero) — *post-classical extension only*, flagged.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Schisandra is not classical in Tibb._ No Avicennan, Ibn al-Bayṭār, or Tibb al-Nabawī attestation. The herb is outside the canonical reach of the tradition.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *生脈散 (Shēng Mài Sǎn, "Generate-Pulse Powder")* — *the foundational formula featuring 五味子*: 人參 (ginseng) + 麥門冬 (ophiopogon) + 五味子, in a 1:2:1 ratio. For *qi-and-fluid depletion from heat injury* — exhaustion, profuse sweating, weak pulse, palpitations after heatstroke or severe febrile illness. Originally from the _Yi Xue Qi Yuan_ (12th c.); now one of the most widely prescribed classical formulas in modern Chinese clinical practice. Modern intravenous version (生脈注射液) is used in Chinese hospitals for shock and cardiovascular emergencies.
- *小青龍湯 (Xiǎo Qīng Lóng Tāng, "Minor Blue Dragon Decoction")* — _Shanghan Lun_; for cold-fluid retention with wheezing and cough; 五味子 modifies the strong-acting warming-dispersing combination (ephedra + cinnamon + ginger) by *astringing* — preventing the pungent-warming dispersing action from over-depleting Lung qi.
- *都氣丸 (Dū Qì Wán, "Capital Qi Pill")* — *liù wèi dì huáng wán with added 五味子* — for Lung-Kidney qi-and-yin vacuity with cough, wheezing, and seminal emission.
- *人參養榮湯 (Rén Shēn Yǎng Róng Tāng, "Ginseng Nourishing-Construction Decoction")* — for qi-blood-yin vacuity with consumption; 五味子 as supporting astringent.
- *四神丸 (Sì Shén Wán, "Four Spirits Pill")* — for cock-crow diarrhea of Kidney-yang vacuity; 五味子 as astringent supporting.
- *Wǔwèizǐ tincture / wine / syrup* — daily-tonic preparations in the modern Chinese pharmacy; widely sold in liquor-store formats as adaptogenic-tonic preparation.
- *Modern hepatology formulas* — 五味子 is in many modern Chinese liver-supportive compound preparations for chronic hepatitis; standardized schisandrin extracts are licensed pharmaceuticals.#footnote[Formula sources: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.; _Yi Xue Qi Yuan_ (12th c., for 生脈散); _Shanghan Lun_ (for 小青龍湯). Modern hepatology preparations: standard Chinese clinical literature.]

=== Korean (the omija tradition)

- *오미자차 (Omija-cha, "Five-Flavor Tea")* — the canonical Korean traditional drink: cold-infused (overnight in cold water — *not* hot-brewed, since heat releases too much bitterness) schisandra berries, sweetened with honey or sugar, often with pine nuts as garnish. *A daily-summer-cooling drink* in traditional Korean dietetics; the cold-water infusion preserves the *sour* and *sweet* notes while suppressing the bitter and pungent.
- *Omija jelly, omija jam, omija liqueur* — modern Korean preparations.

=== Russian / Soviet (the adaptogen register)

- *Soviet pilot and athlete preparations* (mid-20th c. military and athletic medicine): liquid extracts and tinctures of schisandra for fatigue resistance, mental endurance, and physical performance. Studied by Brekhman and collaborators; entered the Russian Pharmacopoeia.
- *Modern Russian commercial extracts* — continue this adaptogen tradition.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

No classical Western tradition. Modern Western herbal: schisandra entered Western herbalism in the 1980s–1990s as part of the *adaptogen* category, drawing on both Chinese-medical and Soviet-Russian research. ESCOP does not have a dedicated monograph; the German Commission E does not address it. *American Herbal Pharmacopoeia* has a monograph. Western use is post-classical.

=== Ṭibb / Ayurveda

No classical formulas.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Schisandra is a *cool-temperate continental East Asian climber* — the principal natural range is *northeastern China, Korea, the Russian Far East (Primorye, Sakhalin), and northern Japan (Hokkaido and northern Honshu)*. The plant thrives in *cool-summer / cold-winter continental* climates with sufficient rainfall — fitting the *continental seasonal-extreme* zone with *cool-damp* admixture along the Pacific maritime fringe. See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework.

=== Continental seasonal-extreme (the home climate)

Cool / cold winters, warm humid summers; constitutional swing wide across the year.

*Traditional preparation.* Chinese-medical use is *pharmaceutical and decoctional* — dried berry in compound formulas. The Korean traditional preparation is *cold-water infusion* (preserving the elegant flavor profile while suppressing the harsh bitter / pungent notes), a *summer cooling drink* — climatically apt for the hot-humid summer pole of the continental seasonal-extreme cycle. The Russian Soviet preparation is *tincture and extract* — pharmaceutical adaptogen, used for stamina and fatigue.

*Cares.* The cool-warming-astringent profile is climatically apt for the cool/cold seasonal pole and as a *between-season constitutional tonic*. Heavy summer use of the warming-astringent form should be moderated; the Korean cold-water-tea register handles this elegantly.

=== Other climates — post-classical use

Modern Western use is climatically agnostic. The classical Chinese register pairs with cool-season and constitutional cool-pattern conditions; modern integrative practice often uses schisandra year-round as an adaptogen with broader applicability.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_)

The modern Western adaptogen register has globalized schisandra use across all climates; the high-dose extract preparations may compound the astringent-warming profile in hot-Pitta or summer contexts. The Korean cold-tea register is the *climatically refined* preparation; the Chinese pharmaceutical decoction register matches clinical-targeted use. See Caraka Sū. 26 for the _viruddha_ framework; see `climates.typ` for the continental seasonal-extreme profile.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Approximate composition (dried Schisandra chinensis fruit, per 100 g):*
- Energy ~285 kcal; Water ~12 g
- Carbohydrate ~67 g; fiber ~15 g
- Protein ~7 g; Fat ~5 g
- Sugars: glucose, fructose, sucrose
- Organic acids (the sour fraction): citric, malic, tartaric, fumaric acids
- *Schisandrin family lignans (the principal active fraction)* — dibenzocyclooctadiene lignans: *schisandrin (schizandrin)*, *gomisin A*, *schisandrin B*, *schisandrin C*, *gomisin N*, *deoxyschizandrin*, *γ-schisandrin*; concentrated in the seed and fruit
- *Triterpenoids:* schisanlactone, schinortriterpenoids
- *Polysaccharides* — immunomodulatory in preclinical models
- Volatile oil: α-pinene, β-pinene, citral, sesquiterpenes (modest aromatic note)
- *Vitamins:* vitamin C (modest), vitamin E
- Minerals: modest iron, manganese, zinc
- Phenolic compounds and flavonoids: anthocyanins (the red color), quercetin, rutin#footnote[Phytochemistry: extensive modern pharmacognosy; standard references in _Phytochemistry_, _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_, _Planta Medica_; the schisandrin family is the principal active fraction and is well-characterized.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Hepatoprotective — the headline modern action*. Schisandrin and gomisin compounds have *robust hepatoprotective activity* in preclinical and modest clinical settings: induce phase-II liver detoxification enzymes (GST, UGT), reduce CYP-mediated bioactivation of hepatotoxins, reduce oxidative stress in hepatocytes. Modern Chinese clinical use for chronic viral hepatitis and drug-induced hepatitis is substantial; schisandrin C is the basis of the licensed pharmaceutical *bicyclol*, used widely in China for chronic hepatitis with elevated transaminases.
- *Adaptogenic / stress-tolerance / endurance* — Soviet-era pharmacology and modern Western adaptogen research support a modest stamina-enhancing and stress-tolerance effect; mechanism involves HPA-axis modulation, mitochondrial function support, and nitric oxide modulation.
- *CNS-modulating* — preclinical and modest clinical evidence for anxiolytic, antidepressant, and nootropic effects.
- *Cardiovascular* — modest blood-pressure-stabilizing and cardiac-rhythm-stabilizing effects in some preclinical and clinical studies.
- *Antioxidant* — substantial in preclinical models.
- *Anti-inflammatory* — preclinical and modest clinical.
- *Anti-tumor (preclinical)* — promising preclinical data; clinical relevance preliminary.
- *Estrogen-modulating (mild)* — modest phytoestrogenic activity; relevant in some hormone-sensitive contexts.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Chinese decoction:* 3–10 g of dried berry per day, in compound formulas.
- *Standardized extract:* 100–200 mg of schisandrin-standardized extract per day (Western herbalism).
- *Korean omija tea:* unrestricted at culinary intake; one cup of cold-brewed tea is a typical daily serving.
- *Tincture:* 1–3 mL of 1:5 tincture, 2–3 times daily.
- *Pregnancy:* avoid therapeutic doses (the strong astringent action); small culinary use generally safe.
- *Concurrent medication:* the *CYP3A4 induction* by some schisandrin compounds is *clinically significant* — may reduce effectiveness of CYP3A4-metabolized drugs (immunosuppressants, some chemotherapies, statins, some antivirals); *combine cautiously with prescription medications and consult clinician*. Conversely, schisandra extracts have been *deliberately combined* with cyclosporine in transplant medicine to *reduce dose* and side effects (the licensed combination preparation in some markets).
- *Initial-stage acute febrile illness:* avoid (classical contraindication preserved).
- *Heat-pattern conditions, peptic ulcer, gastric reflux:* moderate (the strong sour-warming-astringent action).
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.* The CYP3A4 interaction is the principal modern pharmacokinetic concern.#footnote[Dose ranges: Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; AHP _Schisandra Monograph_; modern Chinese clinical hepatology literature. CYP3A4 induction: extensively documented in pharmacokinetic literature; the bicyclol / cyclosporine clinical co-administration is established Chinese transplant medicine.]

